(ns ecc.curve
  "The NIST prime curves — **P-256, P-384 and P-521** — over `Fp`, in portable
  `.cljc`.

  One implementation, three parameter sets. All three are short Weierstrass
  with `a = p - 3`, which is why the doubling formula below can be the one
  specialised for `a = -3`: it is not an optimisation chosen for these curves,
  it is the reason these curves were chosen to have that `a`.

  ## What the corpus does not cover, and what does

  Wycheproof supplies **both** parties' keys, so nothing in it multiplies the
  base point: `gx` and `gy` could both be wrong and all 1,806 cases would
  still pass. Measured — changing one digit of P-256's `gy` leaves the whole
  corpus green and turns only the parameter invariants and the
  `java.security` oracle red. That is why both of those exist.

  ## The parameters are asserted, not trusted

  They were read out of the JVM's own tables (see
  `scripts/extract_curve_params.cljk`) rather than typed from a document, and
  then they are **checked**, in `ecc.curve-test`, against three properties
  that no wrong constant survives:

    G is on the curve            catches a wrong b, gx or gy
    n * G is the point at infinity   catches a wrong n
    a = p - 3                    catches a wrong a, and pins the form

  Behaviourally they are checked much harder: 1,806 Project Wycheproof ECDH
  cases, and — for P-256 and P-521 — RFC 9180's own `DeriveKeyPair` vectors,
  which are the only thing here that exercises the **base point**. Wycheproof
  supplies both parties' keys, so it never multiplies G.

  ## Not constant-time

  `scalar-mult` is double-and-add and branches on the bits of the scalar.
  See the README."
  (:require [ecc.bignum :as b]))

;; ── parameter sets ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

(defn- curve [nm byte-len p a bb gx gy n]
  {:name nm :byte-len byte-len
   :p (b/of-dec p) :a (b/of-dec a) :b (b/of-dec bb)
   :gx (b/of-dec gx) :gy (b/of-dec gy) :n (b/of-dec n)})

